# Project workflow

## Roadmap
- Before starting work, always read the `roadmap/` directory and use the roadmap document for the version currently being worked on as the authoritative planning source for scope, priorities and acceptance criteria.
- Do not infer the current version's status, prerequisites or scope from task records for earlier versions. Consult them only when the current roadmap explicitly requires historical evidence.

## Stack and build
- Minecraft 1.21.1, Fabric Loader + Fabric API and NeoForge `21.1.265` (Forge deferred), Yarn `1.21.1+build.3`, Java 21. Gradle modules `common`, `fabric`, `neoforge` (see "Loader boundary" in `architecture.mdc`).
- Multi-step, cross-package, high-risk, or likely multi-session work must keep a concise durable record under `docs/tasks/`; simple answers and one-step mechanical edits are exempt.
- Build: `./gradlew build`. Each loader's publishable jar is copied, alone, to `build/release/fabric/the_dream_table-fabric-<version>.jar` and `build/release/neoforge/the_dream_table-neoforge-<version>.jar` (each loader's `releaseJar` task, run by `assemble`); these are the files uploaded to the Fabric and NeoForge CurseForge files. The same jars sit in `<loader>/build/libs/` next to `sources` and `dev-shadow` jars, which are for development only. Gradle 9.4.1 with a Java 21 toolchain and daemon JVM (`gradle/gradle-daemon-jvm.properties`). The system Java is 25; `~/.gradle/gradle.properties` sets `org.gradle.java.home` to `/home/davisr/.jdks/jdk-21.0.12.1+1`, so plain `./gradlew` works in any terminal.
- `./gradlew :fabric:runClient` and `:neoforge:runClient` always play as `Davi` (`loom.runs.client` in each loader's `build.gradle`), so the offline UUID, and with it My Farms and table sessions, stays the same between launches. Without it Fabric picks a random `PlayerNNN` each launch.
- After every change run `./gradlew compileJava`. The game can't be launched here, so always say what was not tested in game.
- Automated tests live in `fabric/src/test/java` and cover the shared code (JUnit 5 with `fabric-loader-junit`; call `GameSetup.bootstrap()` before using blocks or items). Run `./gradlew test` after logic changes; `build` runs them too. The first run needs `full_network` to download test dependencies. Add a test when changing pace, ledger, block matching, schematic reading or validation, farm geometry, My Farms listing, or how finished farms are saved.
- CI (`.github/workflows/build.yml`): the `build` job runs `./gradlew build`; the `server-smoke` job (matrix `fabric`/`neoforge`, after `build`) runs `.github/scripts/server-smoke.sh <loader>`, which accepts the EULA in `<loader>/run/eula.txt`, boots `:<loader>:runServer`, sends `stop` on the console once "Done (" appears, and fails if "Done (" never appears, "All dimensions are saved" does not follow, or the log holds an exception or error from `the_dream_table`. The dev JVM can stay alive after a clean stop, so the script ends the run at the save line and kills it; vanilla Yggdrasil key-fetch timeouts are expected. Bump the network protocol (`NetworkProtocol.VERSION`) in the same commit as any payload id or codec change.
- Look up vanilla APIs with `javap` on `~/.gradle/caches/fabric-loom/minecraftMaven/net/minecraft/minecraft-merged/1.21.1-net.fabricmc.yarn.1_21_1.1.21.1+build.3-v2/minecraft-merged-1.21.1-net.fabricmc.yarn.1_21_1.1.21.1+build.3-v2.jar`. For NeoForge APIs use the Yarn-named NeoForge jar under `~/.gradle/caches/fabric-loom/minecraftMaven/net/minecraft/neoforge-21.1.265-minecraft-merged/`. Don't guess method names.

## Versions and releases
- The version lives in `gradle.properties` (`mod_version`) and in the README (version line and jar name). Format: `X.Y.Z-stage` for test stages (`-alpha`, `-beta`), or plain `X.Y.Z` for a full release. Currently developing `1.2.0` (last release `1.1.1`). Alpha and beta each counted from `1.0.0` on purpose; from the `1.0.0` release on, versions follow standard sequential semver (`1.0.1`, `1.1.0-beta`, `1.1.0`, `2.0.0`), never below the last release.
- Test builds stay local. Release only when Davi asks. Commit and push automatically at the end of each finished, verified task or milestone, directly to `main` (fast-forward, never force-push).
- `CHANGELOG.md` always holds only the version in development (it is what Davi posts on CurseForge): replace it when a version starts and keep it current in the same commit as each player-facing change.
- Release only after every adjustment is done and Davi announces the CurseForge post. Release: commit, `git push`, then `gh release create v<version> build/release/fabric/the_dream_table-fabric-<version>.jar build/release/neoforge/the_dream_table-neoforge-<version>.jar`, adding `--prerelease` only for alpha and beta versions. Then point Davi to `CHANGELOG.md` for the CurseForge post.
- Changelogs always start with the heading `# The Dream Table <version>`, using the exact released version with its stage, if any (e.g. `1.1.2-beta`, `2.0.0`). Then a detailed list of every change since the last release, grouped into `##` sections by topic. Explain what each change does for the player, not just its name.
- Never copy jars into the CurseForge modpack instance unless asked.
- Test handoff (standing user decision, 2026-10-29): whenever a build is handed to Davi for testing, after `./gradlew build` passes on the committed code, put the jars in the main checkout's standard release folder, `<repo root>/build/release/fabric/` and `<repo root>/build/release/neoforge/` (not only in a worktree's `build/`). Keep only the current jar in each folder, as `releaseJar` does, check that the copies are identical to the built jars, and give Davi both paths.

## Language
- Talk to Davi in Portuguese.
- In-game text, README, release notes, changelogs and code comments are in English.
- All player-facing text goes in `assets/the_dream_table/lang/en_us.json` under `gui.the_dream_table.*`. Never hard-code UI strings.
